
1. What is OpenShift?

OpenShift is Red Hat's enterprise Kubernetes platform.

It extends Kubernetes by providing:

Enterprise security

Built-in container registry

Image Streams

Routes

Operators

CI/CD support

Web Console

RBAC

Monitoring & Logging integrations


In short:

Container Runtime
        ↓
Kubernetes
        ↓
OpenShift (Enterprise Platform)

OpenShift is Kubernetes plus additional enterprise features that simplify application deployment and operations.


---

2. Why OpenShift?

OpenShift reduces operational effort by providing:

Secure-by-default platform

Automated deployments

Image management

Integrated developer experience

Enterprise support

Better RBAC

Operator ecosystem


Instead of installing multiple Kubernetes add-ons manually, OpenShift includes most of them out of the box.


---

3. OpenShift Architecture

An OpenShift cluster consists of:

Control Plane (Master Nodes)

Responsible for:

API Server

Scheduler

Controller Manager

etcd


Usually multiple masters are deployed for High Availability.


---

Worker Nodes

Responsible for running application workloads.

Each worker contains:

kubelet

kube-proxy

CRI-O runtime

Pods



---

4. Projects (Namespaces)

OpenShift calls Kubernetes namespaces Projects.

Example:

Project
    ↓
Deployments
Pods
Services
Routes
Secrets
ConfigMaps
PVCs

Projects isolate applications and users.

Common commands:

oc new-project payment

oc project payment

oc projects


---

5. OpenShift CLI (oc)

The oc command is OpenShift's CLI.

It supports all Kubernetes operations and OpenShift-specific features.

Examples:

oc login

oc whoami

oc status

oc get pods

oc get svc

oc get routes

oc get projects

oc get deployment

oc get pvc

oc get pv

oc logs <pod>

oc describe pod <pod>

oc apply -f deployment.yaml

oc delete pod <pod>

Trainer emphasized learning oc instead of relying only on the Web Console.


---

6. Web Console

OpenShift provides a graphical UI for managing clusters.

Features include:

Create projects

Deploy applications

View Pods

View Routes

View Services

Monitor Deployments

View Logs

Create Secrets

Create ConfigMaps

Observe topology


Useful for beginners, but automation should use YAML and oc.


---

7. Declarative Deployments

Trainer repeatedly stressed:

> Always use YAML.



Avoid creating resources manually through the UI unless experimenting.

Preferred workflow:

Git
     ↓
YAML
     ↓
oc apply
     ↓
Deployment

Benefits:

Version control

Repeatability

CI/CD friendly

Easier troubleshooting



---

8. Routes

A Route exposes an application outside the cluster.

Internet
      ↓
Route
      ↓
Service
      ↓
Pods

Routes are an OpenShift feature.

Kubernetes normally uses Ingress.


---

Types of Routes:

Edge

Passthrough

Re-encrypt



---

Trainer Notes:

Inside cluster:

HTTP

Outside cluster:

HTTPS

TLS terminates at the reverse proxy.


---

9. Image Streams

Image Streams are unique to OpenShift.

Instead of directly deploying Docker images,

OpenShift tracks image versions using Image Streams.

Benefits:

Easier rollbacks

Version tracking

Automatic deployment triggers

Enterprise image lifecycle management


Commands:

oc get is

oc describe is


---

10. Internal Image Registry

OpenShift includes an internal container registry.

Instead of pushing images to Docker Hub,

images can be stored inside the cluster.

Example flow:

Jenkins
      ↓
Build Image
      ↓
Image Registry
      ↓
Image Stream
      ↓
Deployment


---

11. BuildConfig

BuildConfig defines how OpenShift builds container images.

Supported strategies:

Docker Strategy

Uses Dockerfile.

Developer has full control.

Recommended by trainer.


---

Source-to-Image (S2I)

Only source code is provided.

OpenShift automatically:

Downloads dependencies

Compiles

Packages

Creates Image


Easy for demos but less flexible.


---

Binary Strategy

Developer uploads:

jar

war

zip

OpenShift creates image from binary.

Trainer mentioned:

Useful when CI already produces artifacts.

In enterprise projects (like BofA), Jenkins usually builds the JAR first, then OpenShift deploys it.


---

12. Build Pipeline

Typical enterprise flow:

Git

↓

Jenkins

↓

Maven Build

↓

Jar

↓

Docker Build

↓

Image Registry

↓

OpenShift Deployment

↓

Pods

Trainer recommended Docker Strategy over Source Strategy.


