13. Helm

Helm is Kubernetes package manager.

Purpose:

Deploy multiple YAML files together.

Instead of applying many files individually,

Helm packages everything as a Chart.

Components:

Chart

Templates

values.yaml

Chart.yaml

Common commands:

helm package

helm install

helm upgrade

helm uninstall

Trainer demonstrated packaging WordPress.


---

14. Source Cleanup

Trainer showed why Docker Strategy is preferred.

Poor container image:

Source Code

.git

pom.xml

.m2

Target

Temporary Files

Good runtime image:

Application JAR

Runtime Libraries

Nothing Else

Only runtime artifacts should exist inside production containers.


---

15. OpenShift Scheduling

Developers do NOT decide:

Worker node

Pod placement


Scheduler decides automatically.

Scaling or restarting may move Pods to different nodes.

Applications should never assume a fixed node.


---

16. OpenShift Topology View

Topology visually shows:

Application

↓

Deployment

↓

Pods

↓

Services

↓

Routes

Useful for understanding application dependencies.


---

17. CRC (CodeReady Containers)

Trainer recommended CRC for local practice.

Benefits:

Single-node OpenShift cluster

Lightweight

Local experimentation

No cloud account required


Useful for learning before deploying to enterprise clusters.


---

18. OpenShift Best Practices

Use YAML for everything.

Prefer declarative deployments.

Keep applications stateless.

Use external storage for persistent data.

Push images to registry, not source code.

Prefer Docker Strategy in production.

Use Helm for managing multiple manifests.

Never depend on Pod IPs.

Keep runtime images minimal.



